+# 🌉 Bridge Work Order — [P1] Multi-model chat UI missing: gateway routes 51 healthy provider keys but Studio exposes no model picker (Monica Gap 1/8) (bridge-20261027-audit-gap-model-picker)
+
+## Problem
+The Bangla feature audit (2026-10-27, "Smart AI model" feature audit) benchmarked SupremeAI against Monica's public feature set and mapped 8 missing features. This is Gap 1 — the cheapest, highest-visibility one.
+
+Live-verified the same day: the provider gateway is fully healthy — 51/51 keys healthy, `chat_ready: true`, health-scored multi-account routing across providers (evidence: `health_full_sweep`, gateway snapshot). The intelligence to serve different models is already there. What is missing is the surface: Studio chat offers no model selection at all. A user cannot choose a GPT-class, Claude-class or Gemini-class model, cannot see which provider is healthy right now, and cannot route around a weak or slow model. Monica's most recognizable surface element — the model picker — simply has no counterpart.
+
+The audit's strategic conclusion applies directly: 5 of the 8 missing features are surface/packaging gaps, not engine gaps. This one is the cheapest (~2 hours estimated) and the most immediately visible to a new user.
+
+## Impact
+- Product parity: the first thing a comparison shopper checks (model choice) reads as "less capable" even though the backend is not.
+- Perceived intelligence: a user stuck on a weak/degraded provider has no escape hatch, so one provider's bad day reads as "the AI got dumber".
+- Health transparency: the gateway knows provider health; users see none of it.
+
+## Fix (smallest safe change)
+1. Read-only `GET /api/models` on the API: returns model id, provider, health status (dot: green/amber/red from existing gateway health), and a streaming-capable flag. No new secrets, no gateway logic changes — a thin projection of state the gateway already computes.
+2. Studio chat: model picker dropdown above the composer. Default = current automatic routing (no behavior change for users who ignore it). Health dot per entry; last choice remembered client-side (localStorage is fine for v1).
+3. Telemetry event on every model switch (feeds the eval loop later — which models users actually prefer).
+4. Unhealthy providers are visually marked and excluded from the default rotation until healthy again.
+
+## Acceptance Criteria
+- [ ] `GET /api/models` returns at least 2 providers with health status; payload contains no secrets and no raw key material
+- [ ] Studio chat shows the picker; switching model demonstrably changes the upstream used (verifiable via gateway logs/telemetry)
+- [ ] Default experience for users who never open the picker is byte-identical to today's routing
+- [ ] Unhealthy providers are visibly marked and skipped by default
+- [ ] Bundle impact of the picker on the initial chat route ≤ 15KB gzip
+
+## Reflection Evidence (why + alternatives_rejected)
+**Why:** the audit's shell-first strategy says every hour spent completing the shell beats an hour in the engine right now; the model picker is the single cheapest surface that makes the existing engine visibly smarter.
+**Alternatives rejected:** (a) shipping multi-chat compare first and the picker later — inverted, the picker is the primitive the compare view composes; (b) server-persisted model preferences — requires auth plumbing that v1 does not need; client-side persistence is honest about its scope and reversible.
+
+## Labels
+`bridge:queued` `P1-high` `area:studio` `area:gateway` `gap:monica-01`
